## Why This Is Asked

This tests a standard DP with an adjacency constraint and a limit on picks, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i and t with i-2 due to adjacency
- Reconstruction via a take/parent trace
- Space/time trade-offs; potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonAdjSum(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i >= 2 && dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct indices
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      indices.push(i-1);
      i -= 2;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  return { maxProfit: dp[n][k], indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests dynamic programming on partitioning a string into numeric substrings, including numeric parsing and reconstruction, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts

- DP on string partitions with exact k blocks
- Efficient substring value computation and leading-zero handling
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  // val[i][j] = value of s[i..j-1]
  const val = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(n+1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (s[i] === '0') { val[i][i+1] = 0; continue; }
    let cur = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      cur = cur*10 + (s.charCodeAt(j) - 48);
      val[i][j+1] = cur;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = val[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        if (s[j] === '0' && i - j > 1) continue;
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] + val[j][i];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = j;
    t--;
  }
  cuts.push(0);
  cuts.reverse();
  return { sum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks instead of exactly k?
- How would you optimize for very large n or larger k (space/time optimizations, early pruning)?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests deriving a clean DP where subarray cost depends on endpoints, not internal aggregates, and requires careful index handling and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP state dp[i][t] for first i elements into t blocks
- Transition over split point p with endpoint cost |A[p+1]-A[i]| 
- Backpointer reconstruction for endpoints
- Time O(n^2 k), space O(nk) (can optimize with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minEndPointPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = Math.abs(A[0] - A[i]);
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + Math.abs(A[p + 1] - A[i]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let endpoints = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = par[i][t];
    endpoints.unshift([p + 1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { cost: dp[n - 1][k], endpoints };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if cost were |A[l]-A[r]|^2 or involved a different function?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) time with monotone queue tricks if the cost has structure?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests dynamic programming with turn constraints on grid paths, requiring careful state design and path reconstruction. It evaluates understanding of edge case handling and performance optimization for large inputs.

## Key Concepts

- **4D DP state**: position (i, j), turn count (t), and ending direction (d)
- **Two transition types**: continuing in the same direction versus making a turn
- **Path reconstruction**: using parent pointers or backtracking to retrieve the actual path
- **Time and space trade-offs**: consider optimization for large grids

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(M, k) {
  const n = M.length;
  const m = M[0].length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(null).map(() =>
    Array(m).fill(null).map(() =>
      Array(k + 1).fill(null).map(() => [-1, -1])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = M[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
          if (dp[i][j][t][d] < 0) continue;

          if (i + 1 < n) {
            const newD = 1;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i + 1][j]
              );
            }
          }

          if (j + 1 < m) {
            const newD = 0;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i][j + 1]
              );
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][0], dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][1]);
}
```

## Complexity

- **Time**: O(n × m × k)
- **Space**: O(n × m × k)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to model partitioning with a non-linear block value, a common DP pattern in beginner interviews. Requires prefix sums for O(1) block sums, a clear state definition over prefix length and blocks, and backtracking to output endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP on partitions with exact k blocks
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- State: dp[i][t], parent pointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = S[i]*i;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=t;i<=n;i++) {
      for (let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (S[i]-S[p])*(i-p);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i=n, t=k; const ends=[];
  while (t>0) { const p = par[i][t]; ends.push([p+1, i]); i = p; t--; }
  ends.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], windows: ends};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a trick for monotone optima?
- How would you adapt if windows could overlap or if k is variable?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic programming with a non-adjacency constraint and explicit reconstruction of the deletion indices.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index to enforce non-adjacency
- Exact deletions constraint (k) and reconstruction
- O(nk) time and O(nk) space (can optimize to O(k) with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumAfterDeletions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const keep = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const del = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  keep[0][0] = A[0];
  if (k >= 1) del[0][1] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      keep[i][t] = Math.max(keep[i-1][t], del[i-1][t]);
      if (keep[i][t] > NEG/2) keep[i][t] += A[i];
      if (t > 0) del[i][t] = keep[i-1][t-1];
    }
  }
  const end = Math.max(keep[n-1][k], del[n-1][k]);
  // Backtracking to collect deleted indices would be added here
  return {maxSum: end, deletedIndices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact deleted indices efficiently? 
- Can you reduce space to O(k) and still reconstruct? 
- How does the approach change if adjacent deletions are allowed at a cost?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design with an extra edit dimension on a classic string problem; requires careful transitions for matches, edits, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings (i, j)
- Extra dimension for edits (t)
- Transitions for match/mismatch with edits
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxLPSWithEdits(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[i][i][t] = 1;
  for (let len=2; len<=n; len++) {
    for (let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++) {
      const j = i+len-1;
      for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
        let best = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t]);
        if (s[i] === s[j]) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t] + 2);
        if (t >= 1) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-1] + 2);
        if (t >= 2) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-2] + 2);
        dp[i][j][t] = best;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to require exactly k edits instead of at most?
- How to reduce space to O(n*k)?
- How to reconstruct the edited string and the subsequence?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends the classic Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS) problem by allowing up to k character deletions, requiring a 3D DP formulation. It tests understanding of subsequence DP with additional constraints, careful boundary handling, and the ability to reconstruct the solution path.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Palindromic Subsequence with deletions
- 3D DP (i, j, d) where d represents remaining deletions
- Path reconstruction for deletion decisions
- Time/space complexity: O(n²·k) time, O(n²·k) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function longestPalindromicSubseqWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0))
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
      dp[i][i][d] = 1;
    }
  }

  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], (len > 2 ? dp[i + 1][j - 1][d] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (d > 0) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], dp[i + 1][j][d - 1], dp[i][j - 1][d - 1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[0][n - 1][k];
}
```

